<description>
Recolor every foreground object that encloses a background hole to teal.

Input structure
- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly one foreground color.
- The foreground pixels form separate 4-connected objects.
- Teal is absent from the input and is reserved as the output mark.

Rule
- Keep objects whose bbox contains no enclosed background region unchanged.
- For each foreground object with a 4-connected background hole inside its bbox, recolor all of that object's pixels to teal.
- Leave background pixels unchanged, including the holes themselves.

Key insight
- The changed objects are selected by topology, not by size or position: they are precisely the objects whose mask has an interior hole.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "single-color 4-connected foreground objects on a background; some objects enclose background holes",
  "transform": "recolor each object that encloses a 4-connected background hole to teal; preserve objects without holes and all background pixels"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 9-16 rows x 11-15 cols
  colors:
    background: any color except teal (all eval examples use maroon) [observed]
    foreground: exactly one non-bg, non-teal color (all eval use blue) [observed]
    teal: absent from input, used only in output
  objects:
    count: 2-7 4-connected foreground components
    with_hole: 1-3 objects whose binary mask has an interior hole (binary_fill_holes differs from mask)
    without_hole: 1-4 objects with no interior hole
    pixel_count: 1-17 per object; hole objects typically >=8
    bbox: 1-9 rows x 1-7 cols; hole objects need bbox >=3x3
    hole_size: 1-8 bg pixels enclosed inside the object mask
    border_touch: some objects may touch grid edge; hole objects may also touch edge
    separation: objects are generally non-overlapping in bboxes (minor corner overlap ok)
  foreground_density: 0.18-0.30 [observed]
  transform:
    recolor every object that encloses a bg hole to teal
    leave hole-free objects and all bg pixels unchanged

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (task is classification, not geometric assembly)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size in range. Choose bg and fg colors (fg != teal, bg != fg, bg != teal).
    2. Decide component count (2-7) split into K_hole (1-3) and K_nohole (1-4).
    3. For each hole-containing object, generate a random 4-connected shape in a bbox >=3x3
       that encloses at least 1 bg hole. Verify with binary_fill_holes.
    4. For each hole-free object, generate a random 4-connected shape with NO interior hole.
       Can be small (1-2 px) or larger irregular shapes. Verify no hole via binary_fill_holes.
    5. Place all objects on the grid ensuring 4-connected separation (no two objects merge)
       and approximate bbox non-overlap. Fill remaining cells with bg.
    6. Output = copy input, recolor hole-containing objects to teal.
```
